@PanelNav
$Panel Navigation
This topic is generated dynamically from the action registry.

F8 follows the "Use the trash" setting. When enabled, F4 refuses the
operation if the active file system has no recoverable trash; it never falls
back to permanent deletion. Shift+Del and Shift+NumDel always delete
permanently. Permanent removal is also available as a separate Files-menu
command and can be assigned its own hotkey.
@ConsoleModes
$Console modes (Ctrl+O)
    f4 supports two modes for running shell commands and viewing console output:

  Own terminal (default)
    Commands run in f4's built-in terminal emulator underneath the panels.
    Ctrl+O toggles between the panels and the internal terminal screen.

  Host console
    Commands run directly in the host terminal's primary screen buffer with
    full native scrollback, mouse selection, and job control (Ctrl+C, Ctrl+Z).
    Panels live in the alternate screen buffer; Ctrl+O switches between the
    panels and the real host console.
    When "Show command line over host console" is enabled, f4 keeps its command
    line and keybar at the bottom of the host screen.

@ApplyCmd
$Apply command (Ctrl+G)
Apply command runs a command template for marked panel entries in panel order,
or for the current entry when nothing is marked. Directories are included;
the parent row (..) is never processed. Processed marks can be restored with
Ctrl+M.

Sequential runs one command at a time. Parallel uses the selected worker
limit. Queue runs one sequential batch in the background. All modes execute
on the host that owns the active panel and provide one cancellable transcript.
Commands are noninteractive and receive closed standard input.

The transcript window can be resized or expanded with F5 and may be closed
while commands continue to run. Closing it does not cancel the batch; use
Cancel for that. Send to editor opens the currently retained transcript in a
new editable workspace.

Scalar substitutions are inserted verbatim: quote them in the template when
needed, for example tool "!.!". A leading @ suppresses the displayed expanded
command but not its output. Environment variables are expanded by the target
shell.

Common substitutions:
  !!       literal exclamation mark
  !.!      current name with extension
  !        current name without the final extension
  !`       extension without the dot
  !~       short name without extension (long-name fallback when unavailable)
  !-!/!+! short name with extension (long-name fallback when unavailable)
  !\ or !/ current panel directory
  !=\ or !=/ resolved real directory
  !:       drive or filesystem root
  !?!      description (currently empty)
  !&       selected names inline
  !&~/Q/q  short names / force quoting / quote only when needed
  !@...!   temporary file containing long selected names
  !$...!   temporary file containing short selected names
  !?title?initial! value requested once before the batch starts
  !# / !^  switch to passive / active panel
  ![ / !]  switch to left / right panel

Active-panel !& and list-file tokens run once for the captured selection.
List modifiers: F full paths, Q quoted entries, S forward slashes, A target
encoding, U UTF-8 with BOM, W UTF-16LE.

@ShellNav
$Panel Navigation Keys
  Tab           - Switch active panel
  Insert        - Select file/folder
  Ctrl+Tab      - Cycle workspaces
  Alt+1..9      - Switch to the workspace with that tab number (when enabled)
  Ctrl+W        - Close workspace
  Ctrl+N        - Fork active panel to new workspace

  Ctrl+Numpad + / Ctrl+=  - Select files with the current extension
  Ctrl+Numpad - / Ctrl+-  - Deselect files with the current extension
  Ctrl+Shift+= / Ctrl+Shift+- - Select / deselect by mask
  Alt+=         - Invert selection
  Numpad 5      - View file (also F3; Num Lock may be on or off)
  Ctrl+Space    - Calculate the size of the folder under the cursor (archives too;
                  F3 on a folder does the same: the key bar then reads Size,
                  and when the count is done a message names the folder, its
                  size and how many files and folders it holds, while the
                  size itself appears in the size column)

Extension selection is a standard Far Manager command. The main-keyboard
aliases make it available without a numpad. Matching ignores case and uses
the last extension. On a folder, folders are selected instead. The parent
entry and autofilter-hidden rows are left alone. Ctrl+M restores the last
selection; shortcuts can be changed in the Hotkey Configurator.

@History
$Commands History (Alt+F8)
    The commands history shows the list of previously executed commands.
Besides the cursor keys, the following keyboard shortcuts are available:

  Paste selected command into command line          Enter
  (Left-click acts the same)
  Cancel the dialog                                 Esc, F10

  Toggle substring / prefix incremental search      F2
  Show or hide the command folder column            Ctrl+F2
  Cycle the timestamp column (date+time/date/off)   Ctrl+T
  Insert the command folder into the command line   Ctrl+Shift+Enter
  Go to the command folder on the active panel      Ctrl+PgDn
  Type any character to filter the visible entries
  Backspace to remove the last filter character

  Delete the current history item                   Shift+Del
  Clear the entire commands history (confirmed)     Del

  Copy the selected command to the clipboard        Ctrl+C, Ctrl+Ins

@HistoryViewEdit
$Viewer and Editor History (Alt+F11)
    This history lists files opened in the internal viewer or editor.
Enter repeats the most recent action for that file; the mode is shown in the
right-hand column.

  Repeat the stored View/Edit action                 Enter
  Open in the viewer                                 F3
  Open in the editor                                 F4
  Toggle substring / prefix incremental search      F2
  Cycle the timestamp column (date+time/date/off)   Ctrl+T
  Delete the current history item                   Shift+Del
  Clear the entire history (confirmed)              Del
  Copy the selected path to the clipboard           Ctrl+C, Ctrl+Ins
  Cancel the dialog                                 Esc, F10

@HistoryFolders
$Folders History (Alt+F12)
    The folders history shows the list of previously visited directories.
Besides the cursor keys, the following keyboard shortcuts are available:

  Go to selected folder on the active panel         Enter
  (Left-click acts the same)
  Go to selected folder on the passive panel        Shift+Enter
  Insert selected path into the command line        Ctrl+Enter
  Cancel the dialog                                 Esc, F10

  Toggle substring / prefix incremental search      F2
  Cycle the timestamp column (date+time/date/off)   Ctrl+T
  Type any character to filter the visible entries
  Backspace to remove the last filter character

  Delete the current history item                   Shift+Del
  Clear the entire folders history (confirmed)      Del
  Remove entries with non-existing paths (confirmed) Ctrl+R

  Copy the selected path to the clipboard           Ctrl+C, Ctrl+Ins

    Folder bookmarks live in this list too. Pinning a folder moves it
into its own area at the top of the dialog, above the visited ones, and
hands it one of the ten bookmark slots: RightCtrl+digit then reaches it
from the panel, Alt+digit from here. A pinned folder survives Del and
Ctrl+R and is never pushed out of the history by newer entries.

  Pin or unpin the selected folder                  Ins
  Go to the folder pinned in slot 0..9              Alt+0 .. Alt+9

    The slots are far2l's own, kept in settings/bookmarks.ini, so a file
shared with far2l keeps working in both programs, and the Bookmarks
dialog (F9, Commands) edits the very same ten. An eleventh pinned folder
stays pinned without a digit and is marked with a star instead.

@MediaInfo
$MediaInfo plugin
    The built-in MediaInfo plugin reads common media containers directly
through the active file system, including remote VFS panels. It is written in
pure Go and does not require CGO or the native MediaInfo library.

  F11, Media information   Analyze the file under the cursor
  Ctrl+Q                   Show a fast bounded media preview
  MediaInfo:path           Analyze a path from the command line
  F4 in the report         Open an unsaved .MediaInfo.txt report in the editor

The command prefix, Quick View provider, report language, Inform-compatible
template, and default editor mode are configured under Options | Plugin
configuration | MediaInfo. Quick View deliberately leaves images and text
subtitle files to their native preview providers.

@EnvironmentManager
$Environment Manager plugin
    Environment Manager keeps ordered sets of process variables. Enabled
profiles are applied from top to bottom; later values override earlier ones.
An empty value removes a variable. The resulting environment is used by f4,
future child processes, and idle local workspace shells. Remote shells are
never changed.

  F11, Environment Manager   Open the profile list
  Ins / Del / F4 / F5        Add, delete, edit, or duplicate a profile
  Space / + / -              Toggle, enable, or disable a profile
  Ctrl+Up / Ctrl+Down        Reorder profiles
  Ctrl+Ins / Shift+Del       Copy or cut a profile
  Shift+Ins                  Paste a profile
  Alt+F4                     Always edit the profile in f4's editor
  Shift+F3 / Shift+F4        View or edit the complete process environment
  F2                         Configure ignored variables and editor preference

On Windows, the configuration dialog can import the Environment Manager data
stored by Far Manager 3. It checks the 64-bit and 32-bit registry views and
offers to append the imported profiles or replace the current list. The Far
registry is only read and is never modified.

An ampersand before a character in a profile name assigns that manager hotkey;
use && for a literal ampersand. Moving an edge profile again with Ctrl+Up or
Ctrl+Down creates a group separator. The Always use editor setting makes plain
F4 use f4's editor too.

Use %NAME% to refer to a value produced by the baseline or an earlier profile.
Unix-like systems also accept $NAME and ${NAME}. Use %% and $$ for literal
markers. Profile data is stored as clear text and is not a secrets vault.

Command-line operations:

  envman:                    Open without the external-change prompt
  envman:+Name/-Name/*Name  Enable, disable, or toggle the first exact match
  envman:<file / >file      Import or export NAME=value text
  envman:e                   Edit the complete managed environment

Changes made only inside a child shell cannot propagate back into f4. On a
normal F11 open, an unmanaged-change prompt can restore the profile target,
import the delta as an enabled profile, ignore its names, or cancel.

Macros can call f4.envman, envman, 4D766E45, or 0x4D766E45 with one profile or
file operation string. Opening the manager or full editor is intentionally not
supported from a macro call.

@MouseWheel
$Mouse wheel settings

Options - Mouse wheel settings opens a dialog that controls how many lines
one wheel notch scrolls, separately for the file panels, the editor, the
viewer, menus, and tables and lists; up and down are set independently.

A value of 0 follows the operating system's wheel setting (this is the
default). Any positive value overrides it and scrolls exactly that many
lines per notch.

Other scrolling surfaces (the help viewer, multi-line edits in dialogs)
always follow the system setting.

@PortableSettings
$Portable mode

Portable mode keeps f4's profile next to the program. It is useful when the
program and its settings need to be moved together or stored on removable
media.

The mode is selected before the profile is opened, using an INI file beside
the executable:

  f4.ini          shared by f4, f4-gui, f4.exe and f4-gui.exe
  <binary>.ini    for example f4.exe.ini; takes precedence over f4.ini

The dialog shows the current profile and the mode file. Enabling the first
option writes this setting to the mode file:

  [General]
  UseSystemProfiles=0

The default portable profile directory is Profile beside the executable.
The [General] Profile= option can select another directory. %F4HOME% (or
the Unix forms $F4HOME and ${F4HOME}) means the executable directory;
relative paths are also relative to it. UseSystemProfiles=1 selects the
normal per-user profile.

Copy the current profile when switching locations if its settings, history,
macros, plugins and styles should be available there. Existing files are
kept, and crash logs are not copied. Switching back does not delete the
portable profile.

A commented template named f4.example.ini ships with f4. Copy it next to the
executable and uncomment the options you need. The file is UTF-8; a byte order
mark is accepted. A portable profile has this layout:

  Profile/
    settings.ini, hotkeys.ini, keymap.ini, highlight.ini, farcolors.ini
    history.json, session.ini
    Macros/scripts/       Lua macros (Far-compatible)
    plugring/             installed plugins
    settings/             menus, associations and bookmarks
    styles/, lang/, help/
    logs/                 debug.log from --debug or VTUI_DEBUG=1
    crashes/              crash reports

Lua is built into f4; no separate Lua installation is needed. Put scripts
under Macros/scripts/ and use Ctrl+Alt+Shift+M, or the Command Palette, to
reload them. The profile directory and this layout are also described in
f4.example.ini.

Restart f4 after saving. The mode file is read before the profile is located,
so this setting cannot be stored inside the profile itself.

@SpecCmd
$Special commands
Like far2l's far: commands, f4 understands a few pseudo commands typed on the
command line. f4 handles them itself; they never reach the shell.

  #f4:about#   Information about f4 and the system it runs on: version,
               build, backend, directories, environment and plugins. Also
               Commands > About f4. Ctrl+C or Ctrl+Ins copy the whole list
               for a bug report; Ctrl+H shows or hides rows without a value.

  #f4:config#  The configuration editor: every settings.ini key, marked with
               * when it differs from its default. Also Commands >
               Configuration editor. Enter or F4 edit a value, Del resets it,
               Ctrl+H keeps only the changed keys, Ctrl+A aligns the names on
               the dot. Under the list the key under the cursor is
               explained: its name, its default and the description Settings
               Center gives it; keys Settings Center does not show are
               described in English, as in far2l. Shift+F1 opens the help
               topic closest to the key.

An unknown f4: command shows the list of the commands that exist.

@CmdLine
$Command line
f4 [path1 [path2]] [switches]

Paths come before the switches, or after a "--" separator.

  #f4#                  Both panels open the current directory (on Windows
                      they keep the folders of the last session).

  #f4 path1 [path2]#    path1 opens in the left panel and path2 in the
                      right one; without path2 the right panel stays on
                      the current directory.

  #f4 file#             The file opens in the viewer, as F3 would, and its
                      panel shows the file's folder with the cursor on
                      it. Whether pictures and video open in their own
                      viewers or as plain files is set in Options >
                      Viewer settings.

  #-e, --edit file#     Open the file in the editor.

  #--gui [backend]#     Start in a window of its own.

  #--tty [backend]#     Start in the terminal.

  #--update [channel]#  Download and install the newest build, then exit.

  #--debug#             Write the diagnostic log to logs/debug.log in the
                      profile.

  #-v, --version#       Print the version and exit.

  #-h, --help#          Print every switch and exit.

When f4 already runs in the background (Unix), the files named on the
command line open in that session, and relative names mean the directory
the command was typed in.

@PanelGrouping
$ #Group by#
 F9 → Left/Right → Group by selects grouping independently of sorting.
 Sort modes also links to this menu. No default shortcut is assigned.

 Group by name, extension, size, size on disk, modification/access/creation or
 metadata-change time, owner, permissions, attributes, VFS mode, object kind,
 hidden or executable status. Sorting continues to work inside each group.
 Headings scroll with files but never receive focus or selection.

 Sizes: Small ≤5 MiB, Medium ≤10 MiB, Large ≤100 MiB, then Extra large.
 Change these increasing limits in Settings Center → Panels → Grouping.
 Dates: future days, today, yesterday, two days ago, then month and year.
 Missing metadata stays in No data. Folder size is unknown until calculated.

 Folders separately keeps a first Folders group. Turn it off to include folders
 in ordinary groups, before files. Reverse group order affects ordinary groups
 only: the parent row, separate folders and No data keep their positions.

@ClipboardOverSSH
$Pasting over SSH
  Ctrl+V and Shift+Insert paste what f4 can read from the clipboard. Over SSH
  without the far2l extensions f4 has no access to the clipboard of your own
  computer, and these keys paste only what was copied inside f4.

  To paste text from your computer, use the paste key of your terminal
  emulator (for example Ctrl+Shift+V in kitty, Alacritty, WezTerm, GNOME
  Terminal and Konsole, or the right mouse button or Shift+Insert in xterm).
  The terminal sends the text as one paste, so it goes into the command line
  or the editor in one piece and nothing in it runs as a command.

  To copy to your own clipboard, use a terminal with the far2l extensions, or
  one that accepts OSC 52 (kitty, WezTerm, Alacritty, foot and others; some need
  a setting that allows it).

@ClipboardImages
$Clipboard images
  In panels, Ctrl+V and Shift+Insert save an image from the clipboard into
  the active folder. With both text and an image, choose Save image, Paste
  text, or Cancel. The dialog shows a scrollable text preview.

  File operations / Clipboard images in Settings controls PNG/JPEG output,
  lossless PNG compression, JPEG quality, filename prefix, stem template,
  and minimum digit width. Defaults produce screenshot001.png.

  The stem template accepts Ctrl+G scalar substitutions and input prompts.
  !{prefix}! inserts the literal prefix, and exactly one !{seq}! inserts the
  number. Digit format 000 pads to three digits. The image extension is
  appended automatically. Lists and paths are not permitted.

  The next number is the greatest matching number in the complete folder
  plus one. Existing files are never overwritten. The new image receives
  focus; existing marked files remain marked. Unsupported destinations
  show an explanation.

@PanelModes
$Panel modes
  A panel mode is a set of columns. Ctrl+1..Ctrl+0 switch modes; the mode list (F9, Options, File panel modes) edits them: Enter opens a mode. Each mode can show or hide the focused-file status line independently; an unchanged mode follows the global panel setting.

  Column types and Column widths are comma separated lists that match by position. Columns... and Status line columns... edit them as a list: Ins adds a column, Del removes it, Ctrl+Up/Down moves it, F4 sets its width.
  Empty status line types keep the default status line.

  Types:
    N   name               S   size            P   physical size
    D   date               T   time            A   attributes
    DM  modified           DC  created         DA  accessed
    DE  changed            O   owner           U   group
    LN  hard link count

  Modifiers follow the type, several may be combined (NM, SCF, DMB):
    N:  M  selection mark before the name
    S, P:  C  digit group separators     E  no space before the unit
           F  fractional size, 1.25 K    T  units of 1000, not 1024
    DM, DC, DA, DE:  B  short date       M  month as a name
  O and R after N, A after S/P and L after O are accepted for far2l compatibility and change nothing.

  Widths: 0 is the type's default (a name takes a share of the free space), a number is that many characters, N% is a share of the free space. When the columns do not fit, the last one is shortened and then dropped.
  A repeated group of types forms stripes: N,N,N is three name stripes, N,S,N,S two stripes of a name and a size.

@Player
$Audio player (Ctrl+Shift+M)
  Ctrl+Shift+M opens the player in the panel opposite the active one, the
way Ctrl+L opens the info panel, and closes it again. Tab moves the focus
between the player and the file panel.

  From the top the player shows what plays, the playback bar between two
clocks, the facts about the stream, the spectrum, the buttons with the
volume slider, and the playlist. The bar and the spectrum can be turned off
in ~Settings~SettingsCenter@ (Panels, Audio player).

  MP3, WAV, FLAC and Ogg Vorbis play by themselves; AAC, M4A, Opus, WMA, AMR
and the other formats need ffmpeg.

 #Adding music#

  #F5# in the file panel    Adds the selected files and folders to the
                          playlist; a folder becomes a playlist folder.
                          The playlist keeps paths, nothing is copied, and
                          F6 is refused so nothing is moved on disk.

  #Enter# on a recording    Plays it at once, without adding it. The player
                          then goes on through the recordings of that panel:
                          Down plays the next one, and so does the end of a
                          track. It follows the cursor of the panel only
                          while it plays the panel's recordings or nothing;
                          a track of the playlist is never swapped for the
                          file under the cursor.

 #Keys in the player#

  The cursor walks one column: the playback bar, the buttons, the playlist.

  #Up, Down#                Move the cursor; Ctrl+Home goes to the buttons.

  #Left, Right#             On the bar: seek 5 seconds. On the buttons: the
                          next button, or the volume on its slider. In the
                          playlist: fold or unfold a folder; Left on a track
                          goes to its folder.

  #Enter, Space#            Press the button, play the track, fold or unfold
                          the folder; on the bar, play or pause.

  #Shift+Up, Shift+Down#    Move the item under the cursor among its
                          neighbours. Shift+Right puts it into the folder
                          above it, Shift+Left takes it out of its folder.
                          Ctrl with the arrows does the same where the
                          system lets it through (not on macOS).

  #F7#                      New playlist folder.

  #Del, F8#                 Remove from the playlist; the file stays on disk.

  #H#                       Show the keys on the buttons and in the bottom
                          border, or hide them.

 #Mouse#

  A click presses a button, sets the volume where it lands on the slider
and moves the track where it lands on the playback bar; the slider and the
bar can be dragged, and the track goes where the bar is let go. In the
playlist a click puts the cursor on a row, a click on ▸ or ▾ folds or
unfolds a folder, and a double click plays a track or folds a folder. The
wheel scrolls the playlist, and over the slider turns the volume.

 #Letter keys#

  Which of them work is set in Settings, Panels, Audio player, Player keys:
WinAmp's, YouTube's or both, the default. They work in any keyboard layout.

  #WinAmp#                  Z previous track, X play, C pause, V stop,
                          B next track.

  #YouTube#                 K play or pause, J and L ten seconds back and
                          forward, Shift+P and Shift+N previous and next
                          track, M sound off and on, 0 to 9 a tenth of the
                          track: 0 the start, 5 the middle.

  #+, -#                    Volume, with either.

 #From the file panel#

  #Ctrl+Alt+Left, Ctrl+Alt+Right#
                          Seek 5 seconds without leaving the file panel.

  The command palette (Ctrl+Shift+P) lists the player's commands, as
"Player: ...", while the player has the focus.

 #Playing in the background#

  Closing the player while music plays, with Ctrl+Shift+M or by opening
another panel in its place, asks whether to go on playing in the
background or to stop; Esc keeps the player open. In the background a ♪ in
the top right corner of the screen says that music plays, ‖ that it is
paused. A click on it, or Ctrl+Shift+M, opens the player again on what
plays.

  Without the player the command palette has "Music: play or pause",
"Music: next track", "Music: previous track" and "Music: stop". They have
no keys of their own; the Hotkey Configurator gives them some.
